. For instance, 紫锥花’s impact on midazolam and caffeine clearance was observed after 8 consecutive days of high-dose intake (1600 mg/day), suggesting that short-term or low-dose use may pose minimal DDI risk. Similarly, while 止咳橘红 showed only weak CYP3A4 inhibition in acute settings, researchers explicitly caution that prolonged or excessive use could amplify this effect to clinically significant levels. This underscores the importance of considering real-world usage patterns when evaluating DDI potential, as standard single-dose studies may underestimate risk in chronic users. [Data: Relationships (2450, 47666)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Probe drugs serve as critical indicators of enzyme activity\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The community relies heavily on established probe substrates to assess CYP enzyme function: caffeine for CYP1A2 and midazolam for CYP3A4. Changes in caffeine oral clearance rate and midazolam systemic clearance rate are used as direct readouts of enzyme inhibition or induction. For example, reduced caffeine clearance implicates CYP1A2 inhibition, while increased midazolam clearance indicates CYP3A4 induction. These biomarkers enable precise quantification of herb–enzyme interactions and form the basis for DDI risk assessment. The consistent use of validated probes enhances the reliability of the reported findings and facilitates cross-study comparisons. [Data: Entities (13431, 13433); Relationships (47665, 47666, 2449)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Potential for clinically relevant DDIs exists despite modest observed effects\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Although the measured effects of 紫锥花 and 止咳橘红 on CYP enzymes are generally modest, the potential for clinically meaningful drug–drug interactions cannot be dismissed. Even minor alterations in enzyme activity can significantly impact drugs with steep dose–response curves or narrow therapeutic windows (e.g., immunosuppressants, anticoa